First, we can write $99$ as $100-1$. Then, we can expand $(100-1)^{36}$ using the binomial theorem:

\begin{align*}
(100-1)^{36} &= \binom{36}{0}(100)^{36}(-1)^{0} + \binom{36}{1}(100)^{35}(-1)^{1} + \binom{36}{2}(100)^{34}(-1)^{2} + \cdots + \binom{36}{36}(100)^{0}(-1)^{36} \\
&= 100^{36} - 36(100)^{35} + \binom{36}{2}(100)^{34} - \cdots + (-1)^{36}
\end{align*}

Now, we can see that all the terms except the last one are divisible by 100. So, the remainder when $99^{36}$ is divided by 100 is the same as the remainder when $(-1)^{36}$ is divided by 100.

